WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.300
The kidney's aldosterone receptor binds cortisol just as tightly as it binds

2
00:00:08.300 --> 00:00:13.300
aldosterone. Adult human plasma carries far more cortisol than aldosterone.

3
00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:17.460
A hundredfold molar excess is a commonly cited figure.

4
00:00:17.940 --> 00:00:21.060
Yet sodium transport in the kidney follows aldosterone.

5
00:00:21.760 --> 00:00:24.800
How? The answer is an enzyme beside the receptor.

6
00:00:25.883 --> 00:00:31.863
In 1961, Jean Crabbé used a toad's urinary bladder, which moves sodium from urine

7
00:00:31.863 --> 00:00:33.063
side to blood side.

8
00:00:33.723 --> 00:00:37.463
He cut each bladder into paired halves and gave aldosterone to one half.

9
00:00:38.203 --> 00:00:41.863
After about one hour, its short-circuit current rose above its partner's.

10
00:00:42.783 --> 00:00:46.783
Tracer fluxes in eight bladders showed that the extra current was net sodium

11
00:00:46.783 --> 00:00:51.743
transport. So in this preparation, aldosterone acts on the epithelium directly.

12
00:00:52.917 --> 00:00:58.437
Now the puzzle. In extracts of kidney and other tissues, mineralocorticoid receptors

13
00:00:58.437 --> 00:01:02.957
bound aldosterone and the physiological glucocorticoids with equal affinity.

14
00:01:03.797 --> 00:01:08.677
In humans, that glucocorticoid is cortisol, and it circulates at far higher

15
00:01:08.677 --> 00:01:13.737
concentrations. On those numbers, cortisol should occupy the receptor nearly all the

16
00:01:13.737 --> 00:01:16.557
time. So selectivity is not in the receptor.

17
00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:19.520
The answer came in 1988.

18
00:01:20.340 --> 00:01:24.760
John Funder and colleagues reported that targets such as kidney, colon and parotid

19
00:01:24.760 --> 00:01:28.660
carry an enzyme, 11-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase.

20
00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:31.680
It converts cortisol to inactive cortisone.

21
00:01:32.360 --> 00:01:34.200
Aldosterone escapes this conversion.

22
00:01:34.640 --> 00:01:39.120
So the enzyme lowers cortisol near the receptor, and the receptor is left to

23
00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:43.940
aldosterone. Edwards and colleagues in Edinburgh reached a similar conclusion the

24
00:01:43.940 --> 00:01:44.940
same year.

25
00:01:45.383 --> 00:01:46.983
Liquorice fits this idea.

26
00:01:47.623 --> 00:01:51.963
Eating large amounts of liquorice was long known to cause sodium retention, low

27
00:01:51.963 --> 00:01:53.903
potassium and high blood pressure.

28
00:01:54.823 --> 00:01:56.663
Renin and aldosterone are both low.

29
00:01:57.123 --> 00:02:01.023
That looks like mineralocorticoid excess without the mineralocorticoid.

30
00:02:01.743 --> 00:02:06.523
Glycyrrhetinic acid in liquorice inhibits the enzyme, so cortisol is no longer

31
00:02:06.523 --> 00:02:07.823
inactivated in the kidney.

32
00:02:08.423 --> 00:02:11.223
It occupies the mineralocorticoid receptor itself.

33
00:02:12.233 --> 00:02:13.753
The conclusion has limits.

34
00:02:14.453 --> 00:02:16.993
It came from work on rats and corticosterone.

35
00:02:17.673 --> 00:02:22.233
The epithelial type 2 form of the enzyme was characterized in the 1990s.

36
00:02:22.793 --> 00:02:27.893
Keep this rule. The enzyme beside a receptor is part of what defines a hormone's

37
00:02:27.893 --> 00:02:28.533
target tissue.
